title: HLMM Service Status
agents: hlmm
catalog: os/services
license: GPLv3
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check imports the status of a service monitored by the HLMON
 monitoring system and shows it as a Checkmk service.

 The check maps HLMON's {lastState} to Checkmk states:
 OK → {OK}, WARNING → {WARN}, CRITICAL → {CRIT}, UNKNOWN → {UNKNOWN}.
 Any other value also results in {UNKNOWN}.

 If HLMON reports the service as being in downtime or as acknowledged,
 that is always shown in the check output. Whether it also forces the
 Checkmk state to {OK} is controlled by the "Effect of HLMON downtime /
 acknowledgement on the Checkmk service state" options of the special
 agent rule (default: forced to {OK} for both).

 A second line reports how long ago HLMON last checked the service. If
 the special agent rule configures age thresholds ("Age of last HLMON
 check"), exceeding them raises the state to {WARN} or {CRIT} and shows
 the age in the summary; otherwise (or while the age is still within the
 warning threshold) it is only shown in the details, not the summary, to
 avoid cluttering the main status line with information that isn't a
 problem.

 A third line, "In current state since", shows how long the service has
 been in its current state (HLMON's {lastChangeTimestamp}, distinct from
 the check-age line above). This never affects the Checkmk state, and is
 only shown in the details unless the service is not in state {OK}.

 If available from HLMON, further details-only notices are shown: the
 originating source system, the customer name, and -- only when non-zero
 -- the number of tickets and comments linked to the service in HLMON.

 Each discovered service also carries Checkmk service labels derived
 from HLMON's event source metadata, where present: {hlmm/event_source},
 {hlmm/event_source_type}, and {hlmm/template_ids} (comma-separated if a
 service has more than one template). These can be used to filter or
 group imported services by the underlying HLMON check template.

 If a service that was previously discovered is no longer reported by
 HLMON (e.g. it was deleted there, or no longer matches the configured
 service name patterns), the check goes {UNKNOWN}.

 Requires the HLMON special agent to be configured on a "collector"
 host; services are attached via piggyback to the Checkmk host that
 shares its name with the HLMON host's displayName.

 The special agent rule's "Hosts to import" setting controls which HLMON
 hosts are considered at all: either name patterns (as above), or "All
 hosts" to import every HLMON host regardless of name.

item:
 The special agent rule's configured "Service name prefix" (default
 "HLMM", can be set to empty for no prefix -- useful to tell services
 imported by different special agent rules apart), a single automatically
 added space, and the HLMON service's displayName. If two services on the
 same host share the same
 resulting name, the second and further occurrences get the HLMON
 service id appended in parentheses, e.g. "HLMM myservice (1002)", so
 they are not merged into a single item.

discovery:
 One service is created per HLMON service that matches the special
 agent rule's service name patterns, for hosts that also matched the
 configured host name patterns.
